## Engineering outcome

The course desk update says #4471 closed on 5 October: parser FIXED, `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`; hint WON’T FIX because the booking-v3 form was retired. This is a desk-reported outcome, not a fix implemented or regression-tested in this repository. The historical snapshot and computed evidence below remain unchanged.

## Findings

The 4 October export has 427 rows but 412 unique report IDs merged into #4471. It contains two causes: 379 parser reports and 33 hint reports. These are snapshot counts, not a claim about the current queue. Components below compute counts from the exported data.

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The shared parser reads dates month first for day-first tenants. The booking-v3 form instead displays MM/DD/YYYY while reading input in the tenant's day-first order. Fixing the shared parser alone does not fix the misleading hint. Keep separate engineering tickets, each with its own regression checks.

Resolve each report's tenant directory entry, determine date order with Intl.DateTimeFormat, then use the input channel and tenant form version. Browser locale and intakeTag are intake guesses, not the cause. Deduplicate by ID before counting. Unknown tenants require investigation rather than a guessed merge.

Five reports described as one-day moves remain assigned by tenant and input path in the supplied logic; their wording alone does not establish a third timezone bug. Check the underlying dates before changing that classification.

## Arrival is not merge time

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived from April onward; later bulk merges do not establish when a defect began. Compare receivedAt with mergedAt before describing the backlog or an incident spike.

## Triage decision

The accepted 2024 subject-only decision remains preserved as history. It is over 180 days old. Replace its operational advice with cause-based triage: read the report, resolve tenant locale and form, compare causes, and merge only when both are known and equal. Matching subjects can hide different causes; different subjects can describe the same cause.

<DedupeExplorer />

Recheck these components whenever the desk exports new data. Do not copy old totals into a current-queue claim. The original incident's “one issue, one fix” summary is contradicted by its own computed table.

